class:: HarmonicEnergyRatio
summary:: Spectral power near associated harmonics
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks

description::
Shared interpolated spectral peaks are associated with the nearest integer harmonic of the supplied F0. Only the strongest matching peak occupies each harmonic slot. Missing harmonics retain zero slots; an incorrect F0 or tight tolerance changes the result. The formulas are explicit MIR2 conventions in the harmonic-descriptor tradition summarized by Peeters (2004); this is independent code, not a wrapper around another analysis library. Marks each associated peak bin and its immediate neighbours, counts their squared FFT magnitudes once, and divides by total spectrum power. The three-bin mask suits a Hann main lobe but is not a sinusoidal energy decomposition. Other windows, unresolved harmonics and inaccurate F0 alter the fraction.



classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. This analyzer updates when a new frame arrives and holds its output between frames.

argument:: f0
Supplied fundamental in Hz, for example the first output of Pitch2. Nonpositive or invalid values disable harmonic association.

argument:: maxHarmonics
Literal number of harmonic slots from 1 through 64. Harmonic index is stable when partials are missing.

argument:: tolerance
Maximum distance in cents between a peak and its nearest integer multiple of f0, clamped from 0 through 600. Default 50.

argument:: peakThreshold
Minimum local-peak magnitude relative to the largest included spectral peak, from 0 through 1. Up to 128 strongest peaks are retained.

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192, matching the FFT buffer. Frequency resolution and first-result latency depend on this size.

returns::
Power fraction from 0 through 1; zero on silence.

method:: fromAudio
Convenience constructor: accepts audio and builds Pitch2 plus FFT internally, then calls kr with the estimated F0. The input range defaults to 55 through 1760 Hz, FFT size 4096 and hop fraction 0.25; it accepts the same harmonic controls. Use kr when sharing an existing pitch estimate or FFT chain.

examples::
HarmonicEnergyRatio measures the fraction of total spectral energy accounted for by harmonic partials associated with F0. In this example, it drives an adaptive Karplus-Strong waveguide feedback gate: high harmonic energy sustains resonant string feedback, while breath and noisy consonants cause instantaneous decay. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: tracks the real-time harmonic energy proportion.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var pitch = PitchMPM.kr(source, 80, 1600, 2048, 0.25);
        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var ratio = HarmonicEnergyRatio.kr(chain, pitch[0], maxHarmonics: 16, fftSize: 2048);
        var ratioSmooth = ratio.lag(0.04);
        var delayTime = pitch[0].clip(80, 1200).reciprocal.lag(0.02);

        // Tuned Karplus-Strong feedback gate
        var feedback = ratioSmooth.linlin(0.3, 0.9, 0.1, 1.8).clip(0.0, 2.5);
        var comb = CombC.ar(source, 0.05, delayTime, feedback) * 0.3;
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + comb;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, ratio) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Harmonic Energy Ratio", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 1.0);
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

